          "text": "Thank you for the thorough review. I want to be direct about where I disagree, because I think the stakes of holding are being underweighted.\n\nThe pilot set out to establish whether we could get MMS into pregnant women's hands at scale through government ANC. We moved distribution from around 5 percent to 99.4 percent with no stockouts across 90 facilities. That is the thing we were testing, and it worked.\n\nOn consumption, I accept the register data is imperfect. Imperfect data is what we always have. But the underlying question of whether MMS outperforms IFA is not open. Four separate meta-analyses agree on the direction. We are not being asked to believe something speculative.\n\nWhat concerns me is the standard being applied. We have programs at scale today that were expanded on considerably less than what this pilot produced. If the new bar is longitudinal consumption data of a quality we have never previously achieved anywhere, we should say so openly and apply it to everything, but then we must accept that it will stop much of what we do.\n\nThe costs of holding are concrete and they are being treated as if they were zero. The Federal Ministry put MMS into national guidelines and is asking us about a multi-state footprint now. That window does not stay open indefinitely, and if we signal hesitancy they will move on with other partners. We have developed training for health workers across the pilot LGAs and could easily apply those developed materials to new health workers. And every month we hold, women attending ANCs in expansion areas receive IFA rather than MMS. We have evidence that shows that to be a worse outcome.\n\nI am not asking you to declare the consumption question resolved. I am asking that we not treat an unresolved measurement question as a reason to stop the progression of a program that is working.\n\nHappy to discuss live. I want to land on something we can both support."
